><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Dimension reduction approaches such as fPCA can detect impor-tant characteristics of movement that would otherwise goundetected.•Applying fPCA in conjunction with z-scores was demonstrated tobe a valuable tool for clinicians when monitoring athletic move-ment during a rehabilitation process.•Clinicians and researchers who are interested in this approachcan find software (and a tutorial) for applying fPCA at </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#007FAB;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>www.functionaldata.org</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="0" rect="637.179993,216.935181,821.179993,308.935181"
/></text
